      "id": "00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000012",
      "title": "Markdown example",
      "bodyMd": "---\ntitle: Markdown example\ndescription: A tour of the Markdown Devot understands\n---\n\nEverything below is plain Markdown. Open this note in the editor (_Show Editor_ or _Show Editor and Preview_) to see how each piece is written. The block above, between the two `---` lines, is _front matter_: Devot shows it in a box at the top of the preview.\n\n## Headings\n\n# Heading 1\n\n## Heading 2\n\n### Heading 3\n\n#### Heading 4\n\n##### Heading 5\n\n###### Heading 6\n\n## Text\n\nPlain text can be **bold**, _italic_, _**both**_, ~~struck through~~ or <mark>highlighted</mark>. Use `inline code` for short snippets, and keys look like <kbd>⌘N</kbd> when you wrap them in `kbd`. Superscripts such as x<sup>2</sup> and subscripts like H<sub>2</sub>O work too, and so do emoji shortcodes: :tada: :sparkles:\n\nA line that ends with two spaces  \ncontinues on the next line.\n\n---\n\n## Lists\n\n- A bulleted list\n  - nests when you indent\n  - as deep as you like\n- Back at the top\n\n1. A numbered list\n2. keeps counting\n3. for you\n\nTask lists show check boxes:\n\n- [x] Read the welcome note\n- [x] Open the Markdown example\n- [ ] Write a first note of your own\n\n## Quotes and alerts\n\n> A quote can run over several lines\n> and hold _other Markdown_ too.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Useful information that readers should know.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> A helpful suggestion.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Something readers need to know to succeed.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Something that needs attention.\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> A risk of a bad outcome.\n\n## Links and images\n\nA [web link](https://example.com) opens in your browser, and a link to a note stays inside Devot: back to [Welcome to Devot](devot://note/00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000011). Type `[[` in the editor to link to any note by its title. A bare address such as https://example.com becomes a link as well.\n\n![A sample picture](devot://file/00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000013#half)\n\nPictures you paste or drop are stored with the note. After the address, `#small`, `#half` or `#full` sets their size (also in the image's context menu).\n\n## Tables\n\n| Notebook | Notes |    Status |\n| :------- | :---: | --------: |\n| Work     |  12   |    Active |\n| Reading  |   4   |   On Hold |\n| Archive  |  30   | Completed |\n\nWhile the cursor is in a table, Tab moves to the next cell, and Format Table in the command palette lines the columns up.\n\n## Code\n\nA fenced block takes a language, and a `title` for its heading bar:\n\n```ts title=\"example.ts\"\ninterface Note {\n  title: string\n  tags: string[]\n}\n\nexport function describe(note: Note): string {\n  return `${note.title} (${note.tags.length} tags)`\n}\n```\n\n```python title=\"greet.py\"\ndef greet(name: str) -> str:\n    return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n```\n\n```sh\npnpm install\n```\n\nA block without a language is plain text:\n\n```\nno highlighting, no title\n```\n\n## Math\n\nInline math sits in a sentence, like $E = mc^2$, and a display block has its own line:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 x^2 \\, dx = \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\n## Diagrams\n\nA `mermaid` block draws a diagram:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  Idea --> Draft --> Review --> Published\n  Review -->|changes| Draft\n```\n\nA `plantuml` block (or `puml`) draws UML locally:\n\n```plantuml\n@startuml\nWriter -> Devot: Capture an idea\nDevot --> Writer: Saved\n@enduml\n```\n\nA `markmap` block turns a Markdown outline into a mind map:\n\n```markmap\n# Project\n## Ideas\n- Capture\n- Explore\n## Delivery\n- Draft\n- Review\n```\n\nBoth render offline and are included in HTML and PDF exports. Markmap shows a static, fully expanded map with plain-text labels. PlantUML uses its built-in Smetana layout; external includes and libraries are not loaded.\n\n## Footnotes\n\nFootnotes collect at the end of the note.[^1] They can be named anything.[^second]\n\n[^1]: Like this one.\n\n[^second]: Written as `[^second]` in the text.\n",
